162:
(a) The application for a permit shall be submitted in writing to the mayor and council, or their designee, and shall set forth therein the following information:
(1) The type of license for which application is made and whether the applicable requirements stated in this article are met;
(2) The location of the place of business wherein beer and/or wine and/or liquor is to be sold, and whether the specific requirements related to the location and premises, including, but not limited to, occupancy of structure, land use, distance restriction and all other requirements of this Code are met;
(3) Whether the premises are owned or leased with proof of ownership or lawful possession by a current lease shown by the applicant;
(4) Names and address of all persons, firms or corporations having a financial interest in profits or losses;
(5) A copy of the birth certificate of the applicant showing the applicant to be a U.S. citizen or proof of legal permanent residency in the United States or proof that the applicant is a naturalized citizen of the United States;
(6) If the applicant is a corporation, proof by a certificate from the state secretary of state showing that the corporation is in good standing to conduct business in the state, either as a state corporation or a corporation organized in another state lawfully registered to do business in the state;
(7) Proof of liquor liability insurance, as required by this article;
(8) A signed consent to conduct an NCIC and GCIC background check and fingerprint check to be conducted by the city police department or the county sheriff's office, as authorized in this article;
(9) Payment of all applicable application fees, including all cost of background checks and/or fingerprinting and cost of publishing notice of the application, as applicable, in addition to the applicable license fee;
(10) And such other information that the mayor and council may from time to time require.
(b) The application shall be signed by the applicant and must be verified by oath of the applicant; and if the applicant is a corporation, by the oath of one of its corporate officers or managers. Such application and oath must be on a form provided by the city.
(c) All applicants shall furnish all data, information, and records pertinent to the application requested of them in writing by the city, and failure to furnish such data, information, and records within 30 day from the date of such request shall automatically serve to dismiss, with prejudice, the application. The applicants, by filling an application, agree to produce for oral interrogation any person requested by the police department or the mayor and council as considered as being important in the ascertainment of the facts relative to such application and/or license under the terms of this article. The failure to produce such person within 30 days after being requested to do so shall result in the automatic dismissal of any application pending under this article.
(d) Any establishment making applications for a license to sell malt beverages, wine or distilled spirits for on-premises consumption shall secure and maintain proof necessary, to the city, of general liability insurance for said establishment for liquor liability, injury to persons, and damage to property related to the consumption of alcohol from said establishment in limits of liability with a minimum coverage of $300,000.00 per occurrence.
(e) Upon filing an application, all applicants must pay an application fee and license fee in the required amounts, as established by the mayor and council, the city manager shall issue a receipt and attach a copy of said receipt to the application.
(f) The city manager shall first review the application for completeness and compliance with this article, then the application, along with the city manager's recommendation will be considered by the mayor and council not less than 30 nor more than 60 days from the submission of a completed application for processing any application for a license under the terms of this article.
(Res. of 3-3-2015, § 6-34)

239:
(a) No establishment permitted pursuant to this article shall be located:
(1) Within 1,000 feet of any parcel of land which is either named or used for residential uses or purposes;
(2) Within 1,000 feet of any parcel of land upon which a church, school, governmental building, library, civic center, public park or playground is located;
(3) Within 1,000 feet of any parcel of land upon which another establishment regulated or defined pursuant to this article is located;
(4) Within 1,000 feet of any parcel of land upon which any other establishment selling alcoholic beverages is located; or
(5) On less than three acres of land containing at least 100 feet of road frontage.
(b) For the purposes of this section, distance shall be by airline measurement from the property line, using the closest property lines of the parcels of land involved. The term "parcel of land" means any quantity of land capable of being described by location and boundary, designated and used or to be used as a unit.
(Code 1996, § 10-114)
